<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Privacy manifest. Required for App Store submission since iOS 17; a build
  without one is rejected at upload, which is why this exists despite the app
  collecting nothing.

  Two separate claims are being made here:

  1. NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes is empty, and it is accurate. The app has no
     accounts, no analytics and no storage of anything about a person. The only
     network traffic is the live vehicle feed (an anonymous WebSocket carrying
     the map viewport), basemap tiles, and — only if VITE_ERROR_ENDPOINT is
     configured, which it is not in the shipped builds — crash reports. If any
     of that changes, this array is the first thing that has to change with it.

     Location is the one entry someone will expect to find here and should not
     add. The app reads an approximate position when — and only when — the user
     presses the "centre the map on my location" control, uses it immediately to
     move the camera, and never stores or transmits it. Apple's "collect" means
     transmitted off the device, so this stays empty. What location *does*
     require is the NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription string in Info.plist,
     which is there. Adding a data type here that the app does not collect would
     be as inaccurate as omitting one that it does.

  2. NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes covers the "required reason" APIs the Capacitor
     runtime and WKWebView reach for. These are not our calls; they come from
     the framework, and Apple still requires the host app to declare them.
     Re-check this list when a Capacitor plugin is added or upgraded — a new
     plugin reaching a new category is the usual cause of a surprise rejection.

     @capacitor/geolocation was added for the locate control and needs no entry:
     the required-reason categories are file timestamp, system boot time, disk
     space, active keyboards and user defaults. CoreLocation is not one of them
     — it is governed by the Info.plist usage string and the runtime prompt.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<!-- Capacitor core persists plugin and WebView state in UserDefaults. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!-- WKWebView's asset cache, entirely inside the app container. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!-- WKWebView checks free space before writing to that cache. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryDiskSpace</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>E174.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!-- Monotonic timing inside the app; never correlated with anything. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>35F9.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
